 ## Overview
-Congradient shading is a real-time volumetric shading acceleration technique that defines the surface orientation of a voxel as one of a finite set of local gradients, enabling a fast table-driven mechanism [S2, book]. It solves the performance bottleneck of per-voxel normal calculation and lighting integration by integrating shading into the projection pipeline, generating "congradient values" (concatenated color and gradient data) that serve as direct indices to a precomputed illumination look-up table [S2, book]. Unlike sibling methods that may perform shading in a separate post-processing stage or require complex normal quantization, congradient shading operates as a pipeline with the viewing process, accruing no additional time for shading because the congradient calculation time is much less than the projection time [S2, book].
+Congradient shading is a real-time volumetric shading acceleration technique that defines the surface orientation of a voxel as one of a finite set of local gradients, enabling a fast table-driven mechanism [S2, book]. It solves the performance bottleneck of per-voxel normal calculation and lighting integration by integrating shading into the projection pipeline, generating "congradient values" (concatenated color and gradient data) that serve as direct indices to a precomputed illumination look-up table [S2, book]. Unlike sibling methods that may perform shading in a separate post-processing stage or require complex normal quantization, congradient shading operates as a pipeline with the viewing process, accruing no additional time for shading because the congradient calculation time is much less than the projection time [S2, book]. It builds on [Hybrid Preshading with Postclassification](2171-hybrid-preshading-with-postclassification.md) by preshading volume gradients and associating a quantized space direction index with every voxel.
 
